@startuml
title Completing a partial note — what the token checks, and what it does not
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #4A78B5
skinparam activityBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam activityBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam activityDiamondBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #F5F9FF
skinparam noteBorderColor #4A78B5

start
:a caller presents a commitment
and a value;
:the token sets the completer
to msg_sender;<<#DDE9F8>>
if (H(commitment, completer) in the nullifier tree?) then (no)
  :revert;<<#FDEEEE>>
  stop
else (yes)
  :authorisation accepted;<<#DDE9F8>>
  note right
    This is the only authorisation check.
    The private preimage (owner, randomness)
    is never re-derived or re-verified here.
  end note
endif
if (which context?) then (public)
  :emit storage_slot and value
  in a public log;<<#DDE9F8>>
  :the amount is visible
  to everyone;
else (private)
  :emit them in a private log
  tagged by H(commitment);<<#DDE9F8>>
  :readable by anyone
  holding the commitment;
  note right
    Requires the validity commitment to have
    settled in an earlier transaction: a partial
    note cannot be created and completed in the
    same private transaction.
  end note
endif
:insert note_hash
H(storage_slot, commitment, value);
:the validity commitment
is NOT consumed;<<#FDEEEE>>
note right
  So a second completion of the same
  commitment passes every check. Nothing
  in the protocol stops it, and the recipient's
  PXE has already dropped the pending entry,
  so the second payment is most likely lost.
end note
:the recipient's PXE matches the completion
log and the note becomes spendable;
stop
@enduml
